[6] Synthesis checkpoints
  Euler signs: theorem-level ordinary Lyapunov exponent is 0; the finite
  regression still sees Hardy-Ramanujan sqrt-growth, so it is only a
  diagnostic, not a proof of the limiting slope.
  Random pentagonal signs: positive finite-window slopes in every sampled case;
  the open proof target is an interior zero of the random sparse denominator.
  One early Euler pair flip usually creates visible exponential growth; late
  flips beyond the sampled window become indistinguishable from Euler here.
  The [72,36,16] scalar enumerator exists and is nonnegative; the open problem
  is support/design realization, not the scalar modular-form gate.
